        </div>
      
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-699001462937"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>One special tree — the American chestnut hybrid — was among the dozens of native species we added. The American chestnut once comprised about a third of the Great Eastern Forest in the U.S. It was a cornerstone of wild animals’ diets and a coveted building material due to its very hard wood. A foreign blight effectively made these trees extinct in the first half of the 20th century.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Today, the original American Chestnut tree cannot grow past the size of a shrub before succumbing to this disease, but these new hybrids are more blight resistant. We can’t wait to see how they do.</p><p><br></p><p>In addition to this giant planting project, we’re also working with Blue Ridge Land Conservancy to protect our land using a conservation easement. The easement will be in effect in perpetuity, meaning that, as long as there’s a U.S. legal system, our forest will be protected from development.&nbsp;</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

